#!/bin/sh
# make the Go tests in this tree pass, and prove it
#
# Line 2 is the synopsis, without the name on it, because ply reads it as
# the catalogue entry and the name is already the filename. That is the
# same place mcpbox and edit write theirs, and the same place a shell
# script has kept its one-line description since long before any of this.
#
# This file is the example the field guide points at, and the point it is
# making is that it needed nothing built. A ply invocation already carries
# everything a job needs:
#
#     the goal            the argument
#     the procedure       -s, when there is a skill for it
#     the tool grant      -t, or -sh for the whole machine
#     the model policy    -m
#     the budget          -cycles, -turns, -timeout, -cap
#     the lifecycle       -f, -compact
#     the output contract -check
#
# Typed at a prompt, all of that lives in your shell history and dies
# there. In a file it is named, versioned, diffable, reviewable, and on
# $PATH like everything else -- which is to say it is a capability, and it
# is also just a shell script. Both statements are true and neither
# required a format.
#
# ${PLY:-ply} is the whole trick that makes one compose. Run this yourself
# and it finds ply the usual way. Put it in a toolbox and a running ply
# hires it as a tool, using the binary already in play rather than
# whatever a fresh PATH lookup would find -- which matters under -t, where
# PATH is the toolbox alone and may well not hold ply at all.
#
# "$@" passes your flags through to ply. That is deliberate and it is the
# escape hatch: -B to work a goal the check already passes, -q to quiet
# it, -m to try a cheaper model, -f to resume a particular run. A
# capability that cannot be told anything is a capability people copy and
# edit instead of calling.
#
# Two habits worth keeping when you write your own:
#
#   Bake the budget in. -cycles and -timeout are part of what makes this
#   safe to hand to somebody, and leaving them out means every caller
#   invents their own or gets the defaults by accident.
#
#   Keep the check honest. It runs with the caller's PATH and it is the
#   only reason exit 0 means anything. A capability whose check is `true`
#   is a capability that always succeeds.
#
# Try it without spending anything:
#
#     ply tools  -t contrib/capability     # the catalogue the model sees
#     ply system -t contrib/capability     # what it would be told
#
# Neither calls a model.

exec ${PLY:-ply} -sh -check 'go test ./...' -cycles 5 -timeout 2m \
     "make the tests pass" "$@"
